Title: Measure whether Codex and Pi need the conditional rules
Created: 2026-11-01
Author: reviewer

--- log ---
2026-11-01T09:45Z reviewer created

--- body ---
## Context
Only Claude Code has conditional rules (`paths:`, rules/README.md § Loading
per runtime). Codex and Pi reach a conditional rule only through a skill that
names it. A 2026-11-01 map of the 17 conditional rules splits them in two:
- mechanically enforced (coding-python: ruff and hygiene tests; coding-bash:
  pipefail and shell-call guards; manuscript-build: build gate;
  authoring-skills: frontmatter and agnostic tests): a miss is caught by the
  gate, so the expected cost on Codex/Pi is fix rounds, not quality;
- judgment-only (prose/_all, doctype/*, lang/en, lang/fr, make.md,
  prose-adherence-tests): only review catches a miss; lang/* and
  prose-adherence-tests are named by no skill.
Whether Codex and Pi do worse without them has not been measured.

## Approach
An arena-style A/B, same task, same runtime, rule absent vs supplied
(`pi --append-system-prompt <rule>`, `pi --print` with `</dev/null`; Codex
through the ticket 1413 mechanism or a prompt prefix), each output scored
against the rule by a reviewer that does not know the arm:
1. a small Python script with its test, against coding-python.md;
2. one paragraph of French prose, against prose/_all.md and lang/fr.md.
Budget: about an hour of agent time, a few USD.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Both tasks run on Codex and Pi, both arms, outputs and blind scores recorded under memory/evidence/
- [ ] A finding per rule class: needed on Codex/Pi or not, with the evidence
- [ ] If needed: a follow-up names how the rule reaches those runtimes (skill pointer, injection, or the ticket 1413 global file)
